arage and was found badly damaged in the morning. Though the press cried foul, it was eventually discovered to be the result of a clumsy parking maneuver, rather than anything sinister."] "\n\n" [:p {} "When the event began, it became apparent that the two Swiss bobs were in a class of their own. Having placed 1-2 when the World Championships visited the " [:a {:href "/venues/148"} "same track"] " the previous winter, it became a simple matter of whether the relative youngster " [:a {:href "/athletes/85501"} "Felix Endrich"] " could overcome the 39-year old veteran of the " [:a {:href "/editions/32/sports/BOB"} "Garmisch Olympics"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/85659"} "Fritz Feierabend"] ", pilot of the 1947 World Champion sled."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The gold medal was decided on the first day of competition when Endrich’s team built up a lead of over two and a half seconds. Feierabend closed the gap on the second day but the damage was already done, and Endrich and brakeman " [:a {:href "/athletes/98794"} "Fritz Waller"] " were crowned as champions. The battle for the bronze medal was also closely contested, but was decided in favor of the number two USA team, ahead of Belgium."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Lake Placid funeral director " [:a {:href "/athletes/89451"} "Tuffield “Tuffy” Latour"] " piloted the USA I bob in Sankt-Moritz and, 54 years later, his grandson, also named Tuffield and a former bobsleigh pilot himself, coached the winning American sled in the " [:a {:href "/results/457"} "inaugural Olympic women’s event"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Fritz Feierabend was the builder of most of the championship sleds used in the late 1940s and early 50s."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {} "Run #1"] [:th {} "Run #2"] [:th {} "Run #3"] [:th {} "Run #4"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/85501"} "Felix Endrich"] " / "